WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
A user taps a camera icon inside a React Native app.

2
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.160
Instead of a viewfinder, the screen goes entirely dark.

3
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.320
The application has crashed.

4
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.920
Accessing device hardware on iOS requires two distinct systems to agree.

5
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.320
The application must declare its intent statically before it is compiled,

6
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.880
and only then can the JavaScript layer execute the request at runtime.

7
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.320
If the JavaScript code triggers an API like the camera,

8
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.640
but the Native build lacks the matching XML key authorizing that hardware,

9
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.960
the iOS kernel intervenes.

10
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.040
It forces an immediate, unrecoverable crash to protect user privacy.

11
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.640
Sometimes the application survives local testing,

12
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.000
only to fail in review.

13
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:48.000
If the build succeeds locally but fails Apple's static analysis

14
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.480
because of vague or missing permission strings,

15
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.720
the binary is automatically rejected under guideline 5.1.1.

16
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.480
Fixing these failures requires a precise pipeline.

17
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.600
Developers must map their JavaScript configuration down to the native compilation step,

18
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.360
which then dictates how the application's runtime state machine operates.

19
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.440
Developers coming from the web or Android often assume permissions are handled entirely on the fly,

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.560
applying that purely dynamic logic to Apple platforms guarantees structural failures.

21
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.840
Apple enforces a strict security perimeter,

22
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.880
before a device will execute a runtime permission request.

23
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.400
The operating system checks if the app has declared a specific purpose string

24
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.080
for that protected resource category.

25
00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.680
These purpose strings are stored within the application package

26
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.840
in a file called info.plist.

27
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.920
It acts as a static manifest of intent.

28
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.240
This diagram shows a side-by-side comparison of usage strings.

29
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.800
Writing app needs camera as your purpose string

30
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.120
will get your binary automatically rejected.

31
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.880
Apple's review process requires that the text explicitly details

32
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.680
the exact feature the user gets in return,

33
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.320
such as scan product barcodes for your order.

34
00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.640
Modern iOS divides access into granular categories.

35
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.200
If an app only needs to save a downloaded image,

36
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.720
it only requires right access.

37
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.480
Asking for full read access to the entire photo library

38
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.760
when the app only saves media is a direct path to rejection.

39
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.440
Location tracking demands even stricter configuration.

40
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.120
To track a user in the background,

41
00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.080
the manifest must contain both the background usage key

42
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.600
and the foreground key.

43
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:22.640
They must coexist.

44
00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.320
Even frameworks that run quietly, like Core Bluetooth,

45
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.800
now demand their own explicit usage descriptions.

46
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.280
Any initialization of these libraries,

47
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.800
even just in the foreground,

48
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.960
checks the static manifest first.

49
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.640
Apple's automated review bots do not execute your JavaScript logic.

50
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.800
They only read these static strings.

51
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.160
Ensuring this file perfectly matches your code's capabilities

52
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.880
is the primary defense against binary rejection.

53
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.440
Starting with iOS 17,

54
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.800
Apple expanded its static analysis to target covert tracking.

55
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.840
Apps and third-party SDKs must now declare specific data practices

56
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.280
in a new manifest called privacyinfo.x privacy.

57
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.400
This file tracks required reason APIs, functions that seem benign

58
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.480
but can be weaponized for device fingerprinting.

59
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:10.880
Checking the amount of free disk space

60
00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.720
or reading file system timestamps

61
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.560
now requires formal documentation.

62
00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.160
For standard builds,

63
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.080
modern React Native and Expo SDKs handle this requirement internally.

64
00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.120
Core modules package their own privacy manifests

65
00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.120
that seamlessly merge during the build process.

66
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.920
The gap appears when developers add older

67
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.480
or un-maintained third-party native libraries.

68
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.640
If an external SDK uses a restricted API and lacks its own manifest,

69
00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.920
the developer must manually supplement the main application file.

70
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.520
This mapping table shows how every restricted API call

71
00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.560
made by these dependencies must be directly mapped

72
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.800
to strict alpha-numeric reason codes provided by Apple's documentation.

73
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.560
Missing a single reason code for a buried dependency

74
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.200
triggers an automatic failure in AppStore Connect.

75
00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.400
This shifts the heavy lifting of dependency auditing

76
00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.480
directly onto the React Native developer.

77
00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.040
Writing raw XML files manually introduces syntax risks.

78
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.800
In an Expo-managed workflow,

79
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.920
the app.json file serves as the true source of truth

80
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.120
for the entire application.

81
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.320
Libraries expose Expo-config plugins,

82
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.800
specifically a modifier called with info-lisp.

83
00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.920
This function takes the JavaScript configuration arrays

84
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.400
and translates them into native iOS constraints.

85
00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.360
This flowchart illustrates the pre-build execution.

86
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.080
Running the command executes these plugins,

87
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.640
consuming the JSON data and dynamically generating

88
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.320
the native Xcode project structure.

89
00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.640
The plugin reads the plain text arrays

90
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.600
and automatically structures them into the correct XML keys

91
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.840
and string values required by the operating system.

92
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.920
In this architecture, opening Xcode and modifying the manifest by hand

93
00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.040
is actively destructive.

94
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.520
The next time the project compiles,

95
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.760
the pre-build command will completely overwrite any manual edits.

96
00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.400
To maintain stability in a managed workflow,

97
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.200
developers must step away from Xcode entirely.

98
00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.960
Permissions require treatment as configuration as code,

99
00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.520
residing firmly inside the JavaScript pipeline.

100
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.400
Once the static compilation is secure,

101
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.320
focus moves to runtime execution.

102
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.120
This is the exact moment a user attempts

103
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.840
to access a protected feature for the very first time.

104
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.120
On Android, applications can programmatically prompt

105
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.040
the user for permission multiple times.

106
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.640
iOS operates under a much more restrictive philosophy.

107
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.320
This flowchart maps out the iOS one-shot state machine.

108
00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.600
The system dialog appears exactly once

109
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.080
and the user's response creates a permanent branch

110
00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.600
in the application's functionality.

111
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.840
If the user selects granted,

112
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.080
the state machine records the authorization

113
00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.280
and the API executes normally for all future sessions.

114
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.560
If the user selects denied, they hit a hard wall.

115
00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.000
The application can never trigger that native prompt again.

116
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.200
Surviving this strict architecture dictates a specific user experience strategy.

117
00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.480
Developers build custom pre-authorization screens.

118
00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.800
By rendering custom UI that clearly explains the value of the feature

119
00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.200
before the native dialog is ever invoked,

120
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.160
the likelihood of an initial grant increases significantly.

121
00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:08.880
If the app does end up in a denied state,

122
00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.560
the code must catch that status

123
00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.840
and gracefully redirect the user completely out of the application

124
00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:15.840
and into the global iOS settings menu.

125
00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.960
Because the authorization change happens externally,

126
00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.000
the application needs an active app state event listener.

127
00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.080
When the user switches back to the app,

128
00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:25.680
this listener detects the return

129
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.920
and checks if the permission status has updated.

130
00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.720
The front-end user experience is heavily dictated

131
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.480
by the native security architecture

132
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:33.920
and forced by the iOS kernel.

133
00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.680
The architecture of iOS permissions and react native

134
00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.000
spans from a localized JSON file

135
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.360
all the way to a single unrepeatable interaction with the user.

136
00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:45.600
A three-step audit is required.

137
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.000
Verify explicit usage strings,

138
00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.960
audit dependencies against privacy manifests,

139
00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.200
and verify pre-authorization UX.

140
00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.440
Apple's automated reviewers blindly evaluate the static declarations

141
00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:58.640
in the first two steps.

142
00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:01.680
In production, step three becomes your only defense

143
00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.200
against permanent feature lockout.

144
00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.080
By ensuring the static configuration files

145
00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.520
precisely align with a resilient dynamic user experience,

146
00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.680
developers eliminate the primary vectors

147
00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.320
for local crashes and automated rejections.

